<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gediminids</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gediminid dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gediminids were the dynasty of grand dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a>, who ruled from about 1316. Its members ruled Lithuania and, through the Jagiellonian branch descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Many princely families of Lithuania, Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, among them the Czartoryski, Golitsyn and Trubetskoy, also traced their descent from Gediminas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gediminids.jpg" alt="Gediminids" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Лобачев Владимир. Base: File:Recueil d'armoiries polonaises - COA of Columns.png · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Recueil_d%27armoiries_polonaises_-_Columns_of_Gediminas.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a> was a dynasty of monarchs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> that reigned from the 14th to the 16th century. The origin of Gediminas himself is much debated. Some sources say he was an ostler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytenis">Vytenis</a>, others that he was of peasant stock, and some historians consider him a son or grandson of the Lithuanian or Yatvingian ruler Skalmantas. Most scholars agree that he was Vytenis' brother, though the parentage of Vytenis is explained differently in fake genealogies compiled from the 16th century onwards, and the latest Polish research holds that it cannot be established.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The confirmed rulers began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a> (1316–1341), followed by Jaunutis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> (1345–1377), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> (1377–1434), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kestutis">Kęstutis</a>, Vytautas (1401–1430), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svitrigaila">Švitrigaila</a> and Sigismund Kęstutaitis. The line continued with Casimir (1440–1492), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-albert">John I Albert</a>, Alexander, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a> (1506–1548) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> (1529–1572), and ended with the elective ruler Anna (1575–1586). Overlapping years mark coregency or rival rule. The ruler's title was, by some folkloristic evidence, kunigų kunigas, Duke of Dukes, and later didysis kunigas, Great Duke. Contemporary sources sometimes call Gediminid rulers kings, but Catholic Europe never recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> as a kingdom, and modern historians regard the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a> as the only Lithuanian king.</p><p>A cadet branch founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a>, also reigned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. Gediminas' Cap was used at the inaugurations of Gediminid rulers in Vilnius Cathedral and symbolised the dynasty's continuity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Several other branches ranked among the leading aristocratic houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> into recent times. The Eastern Orthodox branches were at first partly Ruthenized, but most families, such as the Czartoryski, soon became Roman Catholic and Polonized. Others, including the Golitsyns, Kurakins and Trubetskoys, moved to Muscovy and became thoroughly Russified. In Poland the Olelkowicz-Słucki, Wiśniowiecki and Zbaraski families died out, while the Czartoryski, Sanguszko and others survive. Y-DNA testing of living male-line descendants places the dynasty, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a>, in haplogroup N-M231.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gediminids” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Traidenis and later Vytenis the state regained strength, and around 1316 Vytenis's brother Gediminas, who gave his name to the <b>Gediminid dynasty</b>, succeeded to a grand duchy that was ready to expand into the lands of Rus' weakened by the Mongol invasions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
